cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Squarefree integers dividing the product of their shifted prime divisors"
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
version: 2.0.0
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: "Ellena Godoy"
    given-names: "Jorge"
abstract: >-
  For an integer b, let S_b be the set of squarefree integers n dividing the
  product of (p + b) over the primes p of n. This interpolates two classical
  objects: b = 1 gives the squarefree part of n | sigma(n), whose only member
  above 1 is 6, and b = -1 gives n | phi(n). For every b >= 1 the primes of every member are bounded
  by max((2p*-1)b, 2p*), where p* is the least prime not dividing b, so S_b is
  finite for every b; the largest prime lies in an arithmetic progression of
  primes of common difference b, and for odd b parity cuts that progression at
  its first term, recovering the bound b + 2. Membership depends only on b
  modulo n, and the number of residue classes of b for which a given squarefree
  n belongs is exactly the product over p | n of the number of distinct residues
  q mod p with q | n; this is what shows the count |S_b| is not a function of b
  through any elementary formula, since the local conditions couple b with the
  whole prime structure of n. For two prime factors the condition closes to
  pq | b(b+p+q), equivalently b congruent to phi(pq) - 1 modulo pq when neither
  prime divides b, which factors as (up-1)(uq-1) = ub+1 and turns the count into
  a factorization problem. The squarefree members are closed under least common
  multiple, so S_b has a maximum that every member divides. Complete counts are
  given for the 1001 odd b up to 2001. The effective universe E(b) -- the
  largest source-free set of primes, whose product is the maximum of S_b -- is
  measured over 10481 values of b: its size is not pi(pi(b)), the ratio falling
  from 0.384 to 0.0056 across four decades, and grows instead like a power
  b^0.36, matching the fixed point of a parameter-free model whose asymptotic
  exponent is 1/e. A control over a random universe of the same density shows
  the power law is a property of the peeling rather than of the primes, while
  the structural term -- each prime dividing b raising log|E(b)| by about 1/p --
  is arithmetic, with the measured Hardy-Littlewood factor 2.017 against 2. The case b = 1 is classical and is not
  claimed; all results are verified by independent implementations and
  cross-checked against OEIS A187778.
keywords:
  - "number theory"
  - "arithmetic functions"
  - "squarefree integers"
  - "shifted primes"
  - "sum of divisors"
  - "Dedekind psi function"
  - "Chinese remainder theorem"
  - "integer sequences"
